{
  "requirement": "Each language's fingerprint rules determine which attachments\ncontribute to the owning item's structural hash.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.fingerprint` says each language's fingerprint rules decide which attachments feed the owning item's structural hash. That gives three testable things:\n- Attachments the rules include change the hash.\n- Attachments the rules exclude do not change the hash.\n- The rules differ by language.\n\nI read the implementation only to see which attachment kinds exist. `Builder::encode` in `outline/mod.rs` asks the profile's `hashes_comment` and `comment_prose` for comments. Other attachment nodes, such as Rust `attribute_item` and Python decorators, are encoded as ordinary nodes. The profiles show:\n- Rust hashes doc comments and attributes, and skips plain comments (`hashes_comment: is_doc_comment`).\n- Python attaches decorators, and every comment is excluded (`hashes_comment: never`).\n- CSS, Markdown, TOML, YAML and TypeScript/TSX attach nothing, and every comment is excluded.\n\n**Test mapping.**\n- **Rust included attachments.** `hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments` covers this.\n  - Changing the doc text (\"Doc.\" to \"Docs.\") changes the hash.\n  - Changing the attribute (`inline` to `cold`) changes the hash.\n  - Changing the body changes the hash.\n- **Rust excluded attachments.** The same test covers this. A plain `// {cite}` comment can change text and move without changing the hash. Whitespace and layout changes, and moving the item to a different line, also leave the hash unchanged.\n- **Rust parent hash.** The same test also checks a parent `mod[m]`: a child edit changes the structural hash but leaves the identity hash unchanged.\n- **Python decorators.** `python_decorators_attach_to_the_decorated_definition_for_hashing` covers this.\n  - Changing the decorator changes the hash.\n  - Removing it changes the hash.\n  - Whitespace-only changes keep the hash.\n  - A method's decorator changes the enclosing class's structural hash but not its identity hash.\n- **Other languages.** `bundled_fingerprints_exclude_comments_and_preserve_word_boundaries` covers Rust, Python, TS, TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML.\n  - In each, an edited comment plus spacing changes leave the fingerprint equal.\n  - Joining words changes the fingerprint.\n  - The fingerprint is 64 hex digits.\n  - The TS and TSX cases use a `/** docs */` comment. It is excluded, which contrasts with Rust's hashed doc comments.\n  - A Python docstring is content, not an attachment: changing its words changes the hash, and respacing it does not.\n  - A Markdown inline `<!-- -->` comment is text, so it changes the hash.\n\n**Possible gap, not counted.** The three marked tests do not exercise hashing of Rust inner (`//!`) doc comments or block doc comments. Other tests cover that behavior: `reflowing_inner_and_block_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash`, `inner_and_outer_doc_comments_hash_in_separate_groups` and `reflowing_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash`. They are marked for `requirements.sdk.sources.rust.fingerprints`, which owns that language-specific rule. This requirement only asks that the language rules decide inclusion. The marked tests show that outcome varying by attachment kind and by language, in both directions. I found no plausible violation of the subject's text that these tests would accept.\n\nNo `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "2272dba8cdd32c9845b99dc74f77952eb96d2f2046ea64ab9502ddab77190019",
